{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The 'refuted if' clause mischaracterizes the dependence: the ability to compute the repeated-digit complement without double-counting corrections would not refute the dependence of the isolated count on the complement principle — it would merely show the complement can be computed directly. The dependence (and the reduction) is on the *subtraction from the total* (900 - 252 = 648), which remains necessary regardless of how the complement 252 is obtained. A valid refutation would need to show the distinct-digit count can be arrived at without any subtraction from the universe at all, not merely that the subtrahend is computable cleanly.","counterexample":[{"distinct_digit_count":648,"formula":"900 - 252 = 648","refutation_requires":"showing 648 is obtainable without any complement subtraction, not merely that 252 is cleanly computable","repeated_digit_count":252,"total_three_digit_numbers":900}],"target":"2808071ae84ea799392c007bc566f28cde11b365f7391181de7d4a3ea1b868f1"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'refuted if' condition is logically circular relative to the claimed abstraction. The abstraction replaces a positional product (9*9*8) with complement subtraction (900-252). Stating the abstraction is refuted 'if 9*9*8 does not evaluate to 900-252' uses the very equality that the abstraction is trying to establish as the criterion for failure. The abstraction is specifically the *justification* for why these two methods agree; demanding they agree as a precondition for the abstraction's validity conflates the abstraction's consequence with its refutation condition.","counterexample":[{"abstraction_justification":"equivalence of two counting methods","circular_refutation":"refuted if the equality that the abstraction establishes fails","positional_product":"9*9*8 = 648","set_theoretic_difference":"900 - 252 = 648"}],"target":"3bbe2985a5dfb7b17094125251d6051b2bd0d03bb9b1057f3a35dfbbd20a69bb"},{"attack":true,"case":"The refutation condition ('if the zero-inclusion case yields a count other than 144') refutes only the *correctness of the arithmetic*, not the existence of the *shared mechanism*. Even if the zero-inclusion count were miscalculated, both artifacts could still split on zero-inclusion to handle the hundreds-digit asymmetry — the mechanism would be shared but misapplied. A valid refutation of 'shared mechanism' must show one artifact does NOT split on zero-inclusion (e.g., handles the asymmetry via a different method), not that a particular count from that split is wrong.","counterexample":[{"refutation_of_arithmetic":"zero-inclusion count != 144","refutation_of_shared_mechanism":"one artifact does NOT split on zero-inclusion","shared_mechanism":"both split on zero-inclusion to handle hundreds-digit asymmetry"}],"target":"efd782f0a97e6e253e6ecd1bc399a54ae86efdb16ba23323fe0675f6207460fe"}]}